  { line: 'Food and beverage — $963K', drivers: '55,000 food orders at a $17.50 average, built from three channels: ≈44,100 walk-up orders (≈70,000 ordinary on-site parties at a 63% food conversion), ≈6,400 mobile order-ahead pickups, and ≈4,500 express drive-up, office, and school bulk orders. An order is one transaction, not one visitor: a family’s dozen-and-half-gallon and a solo $2 batch coffee each count once.' },
  { line: 'Retail and pumpkins — $280K', drivers: '≈11% of the ~76,400 visiting parties add a curated-retail basket at a ~$26 average (≈ $216K), plus ~8,000 pumpkins at ~$8 average (≈ $64K).' },
  { line: 'Camps and education — $330K', drivers: '10 summer camp weeks averaging 40 children at ~$355 ($142K), ~8,500 field-trip students at ~$14 ($120K), and ~1,250 workshop seats at ~$55 average ($68K).' },
  { line: 'Programs, retreats, and vendor fees — $205K', drivers: '≈ 30 market dates averaging 20 booths at a ~$200 average fee ($120K), ~3,500 ticketed evening and discovery-day guests ($55K), and ~10 daytime corporate retreats or seminars ($30K).' },
];

const lanes = [
  { lane: 'Staffed walk-up counter', use: 'First-time visitors, questions, allergies, and ordinary purchases — the default, fully human lane. No reservation required; same menu prices as every other lane.' },
  { lane: 'Mobile order-ahead with kitchen pickup windows', use: 'Same prices, clearer handoff: guests place food orders before arrival, choose a kitchen pickup window, and collect at a dedicated staffed pickup counter — skipping the order line without creating a second price path or phone-ordering while waiting.' },
  { lane: 'Express drive-up collection', use: 'Dozens, cider half-gallons and gallons, office and school orders, and prepaid bundles — revenue without a long parking stay.' },
];

const gates = [
  { gate: 'Hero-product demand', evidence: 'Customers intentionally travel for the donuts and cider, not solely for a one-time event.' },
  { gate: 'Average food transaction', evidence: 'Sustained $16–$18 average without forced admission or aggressive upselling.' },
  { gate: 'Conversion', evidence: 'At least 60% of visiting parties purchase food or drink on ordinary free-access days.' },
  { gate: 'Throughput', evidence: 'Core orders handed off reliably in under eight to ten minutes during peak periods.' },
  { gate: 'Peak capacity', evidence: 'At least 100 simple transactions per active service line per hour after warmup.' },
  { gate: 'Preorder adoption', evidence: '15–25% of peak food sales placed before arrival or through bulk ordering, driven by a dedicated pickup path — not a separate price.' },
  { gate: 'Repeat behavior', evidence: 'Meaningful same-season return visits and repeat dozen-and-cider purchases.' },
  { gate: 'Labor productivity', evidence: 'Orders per direct labor hour measured in every pilot; the flat $650K payroll assumption survives only if Year-One capacity is demonstrably under-used.' },
  { gate: 'Non-autumn contribution', evidence: 'Education, camps, holiday orders, retail, and limited programs generate $450–550K of annual gross contribution collectively.' },
  { gate: 'Capital discipline', evidence: 'Site and building deliverable within debt service supported by the base — not optimistic — transaction case.' },
  { gate: 'Weather resilience', evidence: 'A poor weekend reduces revenue without unsafe crowding, stranded inventory, or a cash crisis.' },
];

const sources = [
  'County population: U.S. Census Bureau population estimates for Wayne, Oakland, and Macomb counties (2025 vintage).',
  'Household income: U.S. Census Bureau American Community Survey, median household income for Oakland and Livingston counties.',
  'Visitor economy: Michigan Economic Development Corporation / Tourism Economics annual visitor-economy report, 2024 calendar year.',
  'Competitor pricing and models: published venue websites and listings for the 2025 operating season (Blake’s, Wiard’s, Westview) and public listings and customer reviews for nearby cider mills. All figures to be re-verified in a formal pilot-stage competitive audit.',
  'Regional competitive detail: a transcribed 2022 Metro Detroit “best cider mills” video survey by a local real-estate media channel — including Michigan’s ≈87 registered cider mills (second only to New York, roughly 10% of the ≈900 nationwide), Blake’s acquisition of Erwin Orchards, and venue histories — treated as directional context and cross-checked against venue websites during the pilot-stage audit.',
  'Capacity model: parking, dwell, utilization, party-size, and conversion figures are illustrative planning parameters, not observed data; the pilot field program (parking counts, dwell studies, queue and transaction counts) replaces them with measurements.',
  'Construction allowances: regional cost-per-square-foot benchmarks for hospitality and agritourism construction; superseded by a contractor-led estimate before any financing commitment.',
  'Minimum wage: Michigan Improved Workforce Opportunity Wage Act schedule ($15.00 effective 2027); U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics Detroit-area mean wage for food-preparation and serving occupations (~$18.06, May 2025).',
  'Cornman Farms: Zingerman’s public closure announcement and regional reporting (2026) — revenue decline from a ~$2.7–3M peak to ~$1.4M in 2025 against a ~$2.2M break-even.',
  'Food-business benchmarks: National Restaurant Association restaurant operations report (2024) — limited-service medians of 31.7% labor, 32.4% food-and-beverage cost, and 4.0% pretax income as a share of sales.',
  'Cider regulation: Michigan commercial cider licensing requirements and federal juice-processing rules (21 CFR Part 120, juice HACCP).',
  'All P&L, pricing, and volume figures are founder planning estimates, not audited projections.',
];

const roadmapPhases = [
  {
    phase: 'Validation',
    window: 'Sep 2026 – Dec 2027',
    items: ['Brand and demand landing page', 'Maker, school, and customer interviews', 'Autumn and holiday pilot events across two seasons', 'Pilot review and concept revision'],
  },
  {
    phase: 'Site & Design',
    window: 'Jan 2028 – Oct 2029',
    items: ['Property search and due diligence', 'Municipal pre-application meetings', 'Schematic design and cost model', 'Entitlements, construction documents, bidding'],
  },
  {
    phase: 'Programming & Sales',
    window: 'Jan 2029 – Jun 2030',
    items: ['School-program development', 'Vendor and maker recruitment', 'Camp and retreat presales', 'Order-ahead platform selection and testing', 'Operating manuals and training plan'],
  },
  {
    phase: 'Build',
    window: 'Sep 2029 – Jul 2030',
    items: ['Sitework and building construction', 'Kitchen installation and licensing', 'Furniture, signage, and landscape'],
  },
  {
    phase: 'Launch',
    window: 'Apr – Sep 2030',
    items: ['Staff hiring and training', 'Invitation-only operating tests', 'Summer soft opening (Aug 2030)', 'Full autumn launch (Sep 2030)'],
  },
];

const risks = [
  { risk: 'Site or zoning incompatibility', mitigation: 'Municipal pre-application review before purchase; entitlement contingencies; written use interpretations.' },
  { risk: 'Capital-cost escalation', mitigation: 'Civil and utility due diligence early; contractor pricing at schematic design; 12–15% construction contingency.' },
  { risk: 'Autumn dependence', mitigation: 'Order-ahead food and pumpkin preorders with kitchen pickup windows; indoor rain capacity; camps, schools, retreats, and gift markets in the base model — no timed arrival gate on ordinary autumn days.' },
  { risk: 'Event-venue drift', mitigation: 'Weddings, showers, and receptions excluded by policy (the Cornman Farms lesson); no banquet kitchen, event-sales role, or turnover crew; retreats stay daytime and functional; reservations and capacity blocks reserved for special events and parties only.' },
  { risk: 'Brand dilution', mitigation: 'Every program must fit one seasonal signature; maximum visual and attendance density; formal experience review.' },
  { risk: 'Food bottlenecks', mitigation: 'Separate order and pickup; three ordering lanes with one consistent price list; dedicated order-ahead pickup path; preorder bundles; batch planning; throughput testing before opening.' },
  { risk: 'Cider production compliance', mitigation: 'Open with partner-pressed private-label cider from a licensed regional presser; transparent sourcing story; onsite pressing deferred until volume and sanitation systems justify a regulated juice operation.' },
  { risk: 'Seasonal labor shortage', mitigation: 'Early recruiting of students, retirees, returners; competitive pay ($16–$18/hr, tracked against Detroit-area food-service wages); simplified roles and short training modules.' },
  { risk: 'Weather and grounds failure', mitigation: 'Drainage, all-weather paths, covered thresholds, indoor backup layouts, clear closure communication.' },
  { risk: 'Cash-flow shortfall', mitigation: '$300K opening reserve; deposits for camps and retreats; weekly thirteen-week cash-flow forecasting.' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Business Plan"
  description="The Leaf & Lantern business plan: concept, market analysis, operations, financial model, brand system, and development roadmap."
>
  <div class="progress-track" aria-hidden="true"><div class="progress-bar" id="progress"></div></div>

  <header class="plan-hero">
    <div class="container plan-hero-inner">
      <span class="plan-kicker">Concept & Feasibility Plan · Prepared 2026</span>
      <h1>Leaf <em>&</em> Lantern</h1>
      <p class="plan-subtitle">An April-to-December Seasonal Gathering Place in Southeast Michigan</p>
      <p class="plan-thesis">
        Destination emotion. Everyday operating discipline. Built for return visits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container plan-layout">
    <nav class="plan-toc" aria-label="Plan sections">
      <span class="toc-title">Contents</span>
      <ol>
        {toc.map((t) => (
          <li><a href={`#${t.id}`} data-toc={t.id}>{t.label}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <a class="btn btn-primary toc-cta" href={url('/')}>See the Concept Site</a>
    </nav>

    <article class="plan-body">
      <!-- ============ Executive Summary ============ -->
      <section id="summary" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">01 · Executive Summary</span>
        <h2>A small-scale, seasonally defined Michigan gathering place.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The investment thesis: Leaf & Lantern is a capital-disciplined seasonal hospitality
          concept that converts the emotional pull of a Michigan cider mill into repeat
          visitation through exceptional hero products, fast human service, and one flexible
          operating footprint. Unlike attraction farms and event venues, it is designed to earn
          through frequent food-led visits and standardized, operator-controlled programming —
          not acreage, gate fees, bespoke events, or a few crowd-dependent peak days.
        </p>
        <p>
          Stated plainly, Leaf & Lantern is not primarily an event venue, a bakery, an amusement
          farm, or a traditional production farm. It is a modern cider-mill business built around
          repeat visitation, operational excellence, and emotional, seasonal value — expressed
          physically as warm architecture, excellent cider and donuts, a short coffee menu done
          well, a comfortable courtyard, one functional program hall, and curated retail.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two honest qualifications frame everything that follows. First, the claim is not
          “simple” or “low capital” in absolute terms — it is <em>simpler, more standardized,
          and less capital-intensive than attraction farms and bespoke event venues at
          equivalent destination value</em>, a comparison the plan defines with ratios
          (startup capital at ≈1.28× stabilized revenue and ≈$35 per stabilized annual food
          transaction) rather than adjectives. Second, food and beverage is 54% of Year-One
          revenue, rising to 65% by Year Three; education and programming contribute roughly
          30% in Year One. The model treats those layers differently — the food business must
          justify the destination, and programming improves utilization rather than
          compensating for weak core demand.
        </p>
        <p>
          To customers, the same strategy reads as <strong>“More Than Great Cider and
          Donuts.”</strong> Cider and donuts immediately explain the category; “more than” gives
          the brand permission to extend into pumpkins, coffee, markets, education, workshops,
          summer programming, lantern evenings, and the holidays — without ever becoming an
          event company.
        </p>

        <div class="metric-grid">
          {metrics.map((m) => (
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-value">{m.value}</span>
              <span class="metric-label">{m.label}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">Autumn is our heart, not our entire calendar.</blockquote>

        <p>
          The property operates <strong>April through December</strong> in four seasonal chapters —
          Discovery, Gathering, Tradition, and Celebration. January through March, the property
          closes to the public for maintenance, planning, hiring, and limited private bookings;
          the financial model carries those months as cost, not revenue.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Season</th><th>Signature experience</th><th>Primary promise</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Spring</strong></td><td>Discovery</td><td>Nature, schools, gardens, and hands-on learning</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Summer</strong></td><td>Gathering</td><td>Camps, markets, music, wellness, and long evenings</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Autumn</strong></td><td>Tradition</td><td>Cider, donuts, coffee, pumpkins, and Halloween</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Holiday</strong></td><td>Celebration</td><td>Gift markets, lights, workshops, and warm hospitality</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The opening model is deliberately lean:
          one right-sized, adaptable main building — approximately 3,500–5,500 square feet of
          conditioned public and production space, sized for food production, market, and
          programs rather than banquets — plus a courtyard, a donut line and beverage counter, a
          curated market, outdoor teaching areas, and parking sized for timed peaks without
          making the property feel crowded.
        </p>
        <p>
          The plan proceeds only after four gates are passed: parcel-level zoning and utility
          feasibility; a bottom-up construction estimate; demand validation through pop-ups and
          advance bookings; and confirmation that food-service throughput, parking, restrooms, and
          accessibility support autumn peaks without compromising the brand experience.
        </p>
        <p class="scope-note">
          This document is a concept and feasibility plan. A subsequent investment memorandum —
          prepared after the pilot seasons — will add founder and team biographies, ownership
          structure, capital sought, debt and equity assumptions, use of proceeds, investor terms,
          and the expected return pathway.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Concept & Mission ============ -->
      <section id="concept" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">02 · Concept & Mission</span>
        <h2>A real place with a purpose in every operating season.</h2>
        <p>
          Leaf & Lantern is an April-to-December seasonal hospitality and agritourism-inspired
          venue focused on
          family traditions, local food, creative learning, community gatherings, and
          Michigan-made products. It does not need to operate as a large production farm, but its
          offerings remain materially connected to seasons, food, growing, making, and the
          landscape — never a generic rental hall temporarily decorated for different holidays.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          Leaf & Lantern creates welcoming, seasonally defined experiences that help people slow
          down, spend time together, and enjoy the traditions, food, creativity, and natural
          character of Michigan.
        </blockquote>

        <BrandImage
          src="/images/entrance-autumn.jpg"
          alt="The Leaf & Lantern arrival experience: cream hanging sign, stone bases, warm lanterns"
          ratio="21 / 9"
          class="plan-figure"
        />
        <p class="figure-note">
          Representative concept imagery; final architecture, signage, and branding may differ.
        </p>

        <h3>Operating principles</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Business implication</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Seasonally defined</strong></td><td>Every event fits Discovery, Gathering, Tradition, or Celebration.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Intimate rather than crowded</strong></td><td>Timed capacity and comfortable circulation take precedence over maximum attendance.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Food is central</strong></td><td>Cider, donuts, and a short coffee menu are hero products — the core business, not incidental concessions.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Flexible rather than sprawling</strong></td><td>One excellent adaptable building serves camps, yoga, markets, food service, workshops, seminars, and daytime retreats — never weddings or banquets.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Local but edited</strong></td><td>Makers and products are selected for quality, utility, and fit.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Warm, not kitschy</strong></td><td>Natural materials, light, landscape, and real products rather than excessive props.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Professional, not corporate</strong></td><td>Operations, booking, food safety, and service stay disciplined even when the atmosphere is relaxed.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The recommended structure is a for-profit operating company with the option of a
          separate property-holding entity. Educational partnerships may later justify a nonprofit
          partner, but the core venue does not depend on grants to sustain ordinary operations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Market ============ -->
      <section id="market" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">03 · Market & Competitive Position</span>
        <h2>Familiar territory. A different operating philosophy.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The primary market is defined by drive time, not county lines: 30-, 45-, and 60-minute
          bands centered on the eventual parcel in the Northville–South Lyon–Farmington corridor,
          weighted toward western Wayne County, southern and western Oakland County, and eastern
          Livingston County.
        </p>
        <p>
          For context, the broader tri-county region (Wayne, Oakland, Macomb) held approximately
          3.94 million residents in 2025, with median household incomes of $97,760 in Oakland
          County and $103,039 in Livingston County. Michigan recorded 131.2 million visitors and
          $30.7 billion in direct visitor spending in 2024. The year-one plan assumes
          approximately 55,000 food transactions — implying roughly 76,000 visiting parties and
          on the order of 206,000 guest visits, terms the unit-economics section defines
          precisely — plus camp, workshop, retreat, and program participants: a low single-digit
          share of households within the 45-minute band. The precise serviceable market comes
          from the parcel-level drive-time analysis before any commitment.
        </p>

        <h3>The strategy, mapped <small>(illustrative — conceptual positions, not measured data)</small></h3>
        <p>
          The four maps below are strategy maps, not empirical measurements. Positions are
          illustrative, and the reference points are recognizable adjacent business types rather
          than specific named competitors. On every map, the Leaf & Lantern point is a
          <strong>target position — pilot validation required</strong>: repeat visitation,
          eight-to-ten-minute handoffs, 100 transactions per line per hour, and a sustained
          $16–$18 transaction are pilot-stage hypotheses the venture must earn, not attributes it
          holds today. Together the maps express the thesis: compete on emotional value and
          operating repeatability at once, and win on repeat visits rather than maximum
          attendance.
        </p>

        {positioningPlots.map((plot) => (
          <figure class="pos-plot">
            <figcaption class="pos-head">
              <span class="pos-kicker">{plot.kicker}</span>
              <span class="pos-title">{plot.title}</span>
              <span class="pos-flag">Illustrative</span>
            </figcaption>
            <div class="pos-frame">
              <span class="pos-axis pos-axis-y" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="pos-end">{plot.yLow}</span>
                <span class="pos-axis-title">{plot.yTitle} →</span>
                <span class="pos-end">{plot.yHigh}</span>
              </span>
              <div class="pos-canvas" role="img" aria-label={plot.alt}>
                {plot.points.map((pt) => (
                  <div
                    class:list={['pos-point', `pos-${pt.pos ?? 'top'}`, { 'pos-us': pt.us }]}
                    style={`left: ${pt.x}%; bottom: ${pt.y}%;`}
                  >
                    <span>{pt.label}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
                {plot.mechanisms && (
                  <div class="pos-mechanisms" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="pos-mech-title">How the corner is held</span>
                    {plot.mechanisms.map((m) => <span class="pos-mech">{m}</span>)}
                  </div>
                )}
              </div>
              <span class="pos-axis pos-axis-x" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="pos-end">{plot.xLow}</span>
                <span class="pos-axis-title">{plot.xTitle} →</span>
                <span class="pos-end">{plot.xHigh}</span>
              </span>
            </div>
            <p class="pos-axis-note">{plot.axisNote}</p>
            {plot.mechanisms && (
              <p class="pos-mech-fallback">
                Around the Leaf & Lantern position: {plot.mechanisms.join(' · ').toLowerCase()}.
              </p>
            )}
            <p class="pos-takeaway">{plot.takeaway}</p>
            {plot.note && <p class="pos-note">{plot.note}</p>}
          </figure>
        ))}

        <h3>Competitive landscape</h3>
        <div class="competitor-grid">
          {competitors.map((c) => (
            <div class="competitor-card">
              <h4>{c.name}</h4>
              <p>{c.model}</p>
              <p class="competitor-lesson"><strong>Lesson:</strong> {c.lesson}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h3>Nearby cider mills <small>(closest conceptual and geographic comparisons)</small></h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Venue</th><th>Location</th><th>Core model</th><th>Entry</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {nearbyMills.map((m) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{m.venue}</strong></td><td>{m.location}</td><td>{m.model}</td><td>{m.entry}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Nearly every close competitor is autumn-only and free-entry — which validates Leaf &
          Lantern’s free-admission posture while exposing the opening: none offers quality
          indoor space, curated retail, or school and camp programming across an April-to-December
          calendar. Recurring themes in public customer reviews of these venues shaped the concept
          directly: praise concentrates on tradition, donuts, and atmosphere, while complaints
          concentrate on long waits, parking congestion, payment friction, and peak-weekend
          crowding. Those four complaints map to Leaf & Lantern’s throughput design, parking
          sizing, modern payments, and an incentivized order-ahead lane — not timed arrival
          reservations that would block ordinary autumn visits. Competitor profiles are
          summarized from public listings and reviews; a formal audit is part of pilot-stage work.
        </p>

        <h3>What the region rewards — and what Leaf & Lantern deliberately leaves out</h3>
        <p>
          The category is dense and the emotion is proven: Michigan holds roughly 87 registered
          cider mills — second only to New York, and nearly 10% of the ≈900 operating nationwide.
          A widely shared 2022 regional “best cider mills” ranking is a useful read on what the
          local market celebrates, because the winners fall into exactly two camps: scale-and-
          attractions, and hero-product heritage. Neither is Leaf & Lantern’s game — and that is
          the point. This landscape does not change the position; it clarifies it.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>2022 rank</th><th>Venue</th><th>What earns the spot</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {regionalBest.map((r) => (
                <tr><td class="num"><strong>{r.rank}</strong></td><td><strong>{r.venue}</strong></td><td>{r.wins}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The honorable mentions tell the same story from below: Plymouth wins on organic
          authenticity, Alber’s on 150-plus heirloom apple varieties and an 1890 press, Westview
          on two centuries of operation, Goodison on a signature pistachio bread. Two entries in
          that ranking matter most to this plan. First, Erwin Orchards — beloved for 102 years —
          fell off the list entirely because it was sold: part to a homebuilder, part to Blake’s.
          Affection did not underwrite succession. Second, the buyer: Blake’s is building a
          tasting room, restaurant, and expanded mill on that South Lyon site, inside Leaf &
          Lantern’s own corridor. The strongest regional operator arriving nearby raises the bar
          on quality and awareness — and simultaneously makes the contrast sharper, because
          everything Blake’s adds is on the list below.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>What Leaf & Lantern does not do</th><th>Why the exclusion is deliberate</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {exclusions.map((e) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{e.not}</strong></td><td>{e.because}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          Identifying what we don’t do is as clarifying as what we do.
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          The strongest defensible advantages are difficult-to-copy operating assets rather than
          novelty attractions: consistent hospitality, a recognizable visual environment, reliable
          food quality, relationships with schools and makers, a strong email audience, and a
          calendar customers learn to anticipate.
        </p>

        <h3>The metrics that follow from the thesis</h3>
        <p>
          Success is framed around repeatability and profitable spend rather than attendance
          alone. Attendance is not irrelevant — the model does not work empty — but it is
          treated as an input, not the objective:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Lens</th><th>What gets measured</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {thesisMetrics.map((m) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{m.lens}</strong></td><td>{m.measures}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          Attendance is an input. Repeatability and profitable spend are the objective.
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          The pilot-stage validation gates in the roadmap operationalize the first cut of these
          measures — average transaction, conversion, throughput, preorder share, and repeat
          behavior — and the unit-economics section defines the full eight-metric dashboard,
          with denominators, that becomes the operating scorecard after opening.
        </p>

        <h3>The competitive conclusion</h3>
        <p>
          Leaf & Lantern does not seek whitespace by inventing a new category. It occupies
          familiar territory — a Michigan cider mill — but applies a different operating
          philosophy. The opportunity is to preserve the seasonal emotion customers already want
          while systematically removing the friction, crowd dependence, complexity, and
          inconsistent service that often accompany it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every subsequent section of this plan — building footprint, menu, technology, staffing,
          programming, capital budget, and financial model — is tested against that thesis.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Operations ============ -->
      <section id="operations" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">04 · Offerings & Operations</span>
        <h2>Two layers: a recurring base, and signature seasonal programs.</h2>
        <p>
          The base business — hero products, market, grounds, education, and workshops — runs
          underneath a limited number of signature seasonal programs that create urgency without
          turning every weekend into a festival.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Anchor offerings</th><th>Principal revenue</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Spring · Discovery</strong></td><td>School field trips, homeschool days, garden and pollinator workshops, nature walks, Earth Day market</td><td>Field-trip fees, workshops, café, retail kits, vendor fees</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Summer · Gathering</strong></td><td>Science camps, morning yoga, acoustic evenings, small markets, local-food nights, daytime retreats</td><td>Camps, class passes, F&B, markets, retreat packages</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Autumn · Tradition</strong></td><td>Cider, donuts, coffee, pumpkins, harvest market, Halloween party, lantern evenings</td><td>F&B, pumpkins, retail, ticketed evenings, vendor fees</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Holiday · Celebration</strong></td><td>Indoor gift market, wreath and ornament workshops, bakery boxes, holiday preorders, shopping evenings</td><td>Vendor fees, retail, F&B, preorders, workshops</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <BrandImage
          src="/images/learning-hall.jpg"
          alt="The main hall in two configurations: hands-on learning and warm market hospitality"
          ratio="21 / 9"
          class="plan-figure"
        />
        <p class="figure-note">
          One adaptable hall is the operating thesis: the same timber room serves camps, yoga,
          markets, food service, workshops, seminars, and daytime retreats across the calendar.
          Representative concept imagery; final architecture, signage, and branding may differ.
        </p>

        <h3>Operator-controlled programming, not an event venue</h3>
        <p>
          Programming remains part of the model; the distinction that matters to the investment
          case is who controls it. Camps, school field trips, yoga, seminars, corporate
          retreats, wreath and craft workshops, maker markets, and ticketed lantern evenings are
          operator-controlled programming: they reuse the same staff, hall, kitchen, and
          grounds, run on Leaf & Lantern’s calendar, and can be standardized and repeated.
          Weddings, showers, catered receptions, and highly customized private events are a
          different business — planning labor, furniture and catering requirements, capital
          intensity, and brand and operational complexity — and are excluded by policy rather
          than carried anywhere as a revenue assumption. There is no banquet kitchen,
          event-sales staff, or turnover crew. Zingerman’s Cornman Farms is the reason stated
          plainly: even the region’s most sophisticated hospitality operator could not make the
          private-event model carry a premium farm property. Retreats and seminars book weekday
          daytime blocks or scheduled closed days; camps and yoga hold fixed morning blocks;
          public hours are never displaced.
        </p>

        <h3>Staffing</h3>
        <p>
          The permanent team is organized around six functions, not ten job titles — several
          owner-led or combined in the early years: general and financial management;
          food-production leadership (a donut and food-production manager, not a broad-menu
          executive baker — secondary pastries come from approved partners); guest operations
          (counter, pickup, parking, service training); education and programs; market and
          merchandising; and facilities and grounds. There is deliberately no event-sales or
          banquet role. A part-time café and market crew covers daily service across the
          operating season, and peak autumn adds thirty-five to fifty-five seasonal employees.
        </p>
        <p>
          That structure — roughly $300–330K in salaried payroll, $110–130K in season-long
          part-time wages, and $120–140K in peak seasonal wages, plus employer taxes and
          benefits — is what the $650,000 payroll line supports. Wage assumptions stay
          conservative: Michigan’s minimum wage reaches $15 in 2027 and the Detroit-area mean
          for food-preparation occupations was already ~$18 in 2025, so the modeled $16–$18
          hiring range is treated as market rate, not a premium.
        </p>

        <h3>Cider without building a juice plant</h3>
        <p>
          Pressing cider commercially is a regulated production competency: Michigan licensing,
          federal juice-processing (HACCP) compliance, trained safe-cider staff, and approved
          treatment. The plan phases it. Opening cider is an exclusive private label pressed by a
          licensed regional partner to Leaf & Lantern’s specification, with the orchard source,
          apple blend, pressing schedule, and treatment publicly transparent. An onsite press is
          added only when gallon volume, staffing, and sanitation systems justify the investment.
          Franklin and Plymouth show that guests care about seeing where cider comes from — the
          sourcing story must be exceptionally visible until pressing moves onsite.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Unit Economics & Capacity ============ -->
      <section id="economics" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">05 · Unit Economics & Capacity</span>
        <h2>Transactions cannot replace physical capacity.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The model is deliberately transaction-driven rather than attendance-driven — but
          parking, dwell time, party size, conversion, production, checkout, restrooms, and
          circulation collectively determine how many transactions the property can physically
          support. This section states the capacity math in one consistent vocabulary, shows the
          daily bridge behind the 55,000-transaction year, and defines the dashboard that will
          replace every assumption with pilot measurements.
        </p>

        <h3>One vocabulary, used everywhere</h3>
        <p>
          Every volume figure in this plan uses the following definitions. Conversion is always
          measured on parties — a family of five that buys one dozen is one converted party, one
          food order, and five guest visits.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {unitTerms.map((t) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{t.term}</strong></td><td>{t.def}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>The capacity equation</h3>
        <p>
          Daily on-site volume follows one chain: <strong>parking spaces × (open hours ÷ average
          dwell) × occupied utilization = arriving parties</strong>; parties × guests per vehicle
          = guest visits; parties × food conversion (plus express orders that never consume a
          normal parking stay) = food orders; orders × average order = food revenue. The
          envelope below applies that chain at a lower, base, and upper operating case — it is a
          <em>capacity range, not a demand forecast</em>.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Capacity input</th><th class="num">Lower case</th><th class="num">Illustrative base</th><th class="num">Upper operating case</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {capacityInputs.map((r) => (
                <tr><td>{r.input}</td><td class="num">{r.low}</td><td class="num">{r.base}</td><td class="num">{r.high}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Resulting daily capacity</th><th class="num">Lower case</th><th class="num">Illustrative base</th><th class="num">Upper operating case</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {capacityOutputs.map((r) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{r.output}</strong></td><td class="num">{r.low}</td><td class="num">{r.base}</td><td class="num">{r.high}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The base case works through as ≈525 arriving parties, ≈1,416 guest visits, ≈341 food
          orders, and ≈$5,967 of food revenue — about <strong>$6.50 of food revenue per occupied
          parking-space-hour</strong>, the single number that integrates dwell, conversion, and
          basket size. The upper case is a very busy day: at more than 2,600 guest visits,
          ingress, restrooms, order capture, and circulation must all still function, and the
          property would not feel “small-scale” even though the building is modest.
        </p>

        <h3>The daily bridge behind 55,000 transactions</h3>
        <p>
          The Year-One food line is not one number; it is a day-type model across roughly 195
          public operating days (April–December, with reduced early-week schedules outside
          peak). Stating it this way lets management — and investors — ask the right question:
          are 650 peak-day, 350 shoulder-day, and 165 ordinary-day transactions physically and
          commercially defensible?
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Operating-day type</th><th class="num">Days</th><th class="num">Avg. food transactions</th><th class="num">Annual transactions</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {dayTypes.map((d) => (
                <tr><td>{d.type}</td><td class="num">{d.days}</td><td class="num">{d.avg}</td><td class="num">{d.annual}</td></tr>
              ))}
              <tr class="total"><td>Total</td><td class="num">195</td><td class="num"></td><td class="num">≈55,050</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          At the $17.50 average, that produces the ≈$963K Year-One food-and-beverage line. The
          implied physical load is disclosed rather than hidden: ≈76,400 visiting parties and on
          the order of 206,000 annual guest visits at 2.7 guests per vehicle — materially more
          activity than “55,000 transactions” sounds like, and the reason parking and dwell get
          their own analysis below.
        </p>

        <h3>Why express channels matter more than preorders</h3>
        <p>
          A 650-transaction peak day exposes the constraint. At 65% conversion, 650 on-site
          orders imply ≈1,000 arriving parties; at a 1.75-hour dwell they consume ≈1,750 occupied
          parking-space-hours — yet even 180 spaces over 8.5 hours at 85% utilization supply only
          ≈1,300. Roughly a quarter of peak transactions must therefore arrive through genuinely
          low-dwell channels: express drive-up collection, office and school bulk orders, and
          true grab-and-go. That is why the ordering-lane design treats express collection as a
          capacity tool, not a convenience feature.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          Order-ahead smooths kitchen demand. It does not create parking capacity when preorder
          guests park and stay as long as everyone else. Express and bulk channels do.
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Labor productivity: the hidden underwriting assumption</h3>
        <p>
          The P&L holds payroll flat at $650K while annual food transactions rise 54.5%, from
          55,000 to 85,000 — payroll falls from 36.6% to 28.2% of revenue with no absolute
          increase. That is plausible only if Year One carries substantial unused capacity, and
          it is the plan’s single largest operating-leverage assumption. For scale, National
          Restaurant Association 2024 medians put limited-service labor and benefits at 31.7% of
          sales (30.0% among profitable operators) — a few payroll points decide the bottom
          line. The assumption is therefore gated, not assumed: <strong>orders per direct labor
          hour</strong> is measured in every pilot, and a payroll-scales-with-orders downside
          case accompanies any financing model.
        </p>

        <h3>Capital intensity, defined with denominators</h3>
        <p>
          “Lower capital than attraction and event competitors” is a claim that needs a
          denominator. A $2.95M startup budget excluding land is not automatically “low
          capital” — at the Year-Three forecast it equals roughly 1.28× annual revenue. These are the ratios the plan tracks, to be
          replaced with contractor- and equipment-supported figures before financing:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Ratio</th><th class="num">Current plan</th><th class="num">Arithmetic</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {capitalRatios.map((r) => (
                <tr><td>{r.ratio}</td><td class="num"><strong>{r.value}</strong></td><td class="num">{r.note}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>The eight-metric operating dashboard</h3>
        <p>
          Each metric carries a precise denominator, gets a baseline from the pilot seasons, and
          is reported separately for autumn peaks, ordinary public days, and programs. Peak
          handoff time, order accuracy, abandonment, stockouts, and parking queue time remain
          service-level measures beneath these — with handoff and throughput judged at the
          ninetieth percentile, not the average, because averages conceal exactly the periods
          when crowding damages the brand.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Definition</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {dashboard.map((m) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{m.metric}</strong></td><td>{m.def}</td><td>{m.why}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The dashboard is connected, not a list: repeat households × visits per household ×
          conversion × average transaction = core food revenue; orders ÷ direct labor hours =
          labor productivity; revenue ÷ occupied parking-space-hours = site productivity.
          Underperformance can then be diagnosed — insufficient household reach, weak return
          frequency, low conversion, small baskets, excessive dwell, or labor inefficiency —
          rather than debated. NPS is treated as a diagnostic alongside observed return
          behavior, never as the primary proof of loyalty.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Financials ============ -->
      <section id="financials" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">06 · Revenue Streams & Financial Plan</span>
        <h2>Free to enter. Worth paying for.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Admission is always free — grounds, market, café, and pumpkin selection, every day,
          including peak autumn weekends. There is no gate fee, no arrival reservation, and no
          upsell ladder. Ordinary autumn operations stay open walk-up: guests arrive when they
          like. Margin lives in hero products bought many ways — cider, donuts, a short coffee
          menu, bundles, and order-ahead — plus retail and genuinely bounded programs: ticketed
          evenings, workshops, camps, field trips, daytime retreats, and private parties for
          special events only.
        </p>
        <p>
          Peak autumn logistics are smoothed by order-ahead, not by blocking the gate. Guests who
          order food and drink before they arrive choose a kitchen pickup window and collect at a
          dedicated pickup counter — skipping the order line on the same menu prices as walk-up.
          There is no order-ahead discount: a second price invites phone-ordering in the walk-up
          line and muddies a simple, consistent counter. The incentive is the workflow itself —
          known pickup time, fresher batching for the kitchen, and a clear path to handoff —
          while the grounds stay open without timed entry. Reservations and capacity blocks are
          reserved for special events and parties only. A $2 mini coffee and a bag of donuts is a
          complete, welcome visit — the model treats that family as the beginning of a
          relationship that later books a camp, a field trip, or a corporate retreat, not as a
          missed ticket sale.
        </p>

        <h3>Three economic layers, underwritten differently</h3>
        <p>
          Food and beverage is 54% of Year-One revenue and 65% by Year Three; camps, education,
          programs, retreats, and vendor fees contribute roughly 30% in Year One. The plan does
          not pretend otherwise — it assigns each layer a distinct underwriting role, and the
          downside case reduces camp, education, market, and retreat revenue by 50% to test
          whether the permanent-site model still covers property-level operating costs and a
          meaningful portion of debt service without them.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Economic layer</th><th>Strategic role</th><th>Underwriting treatment</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {economicLayers.map((l) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{l.layer}</strong></td><td>{l.role}</td><td>{l.treatment}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          The food business must justify the destination. Programming improves utilization — it
          does not rescue weak core demand.
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Hero products, sold many ways</h3>
        <p>
          The autumn menu is a production system of roughly twelve purchase buttons: donuts
          (single, half-dozen, dozen), cider (cup, half-gallon, gallon), a $2 small batch coffee,
          a double espresso, an Americano, hot chocolate, two family bundles, and one rotating
          item — no milk-steaming matrix, syrup ladder, or size tree. A staff-operated
          superautomatic espresso machine keeps the coffee automated behind the counter while the
          hospitality stays human in front of it. This is not competing with bakeries: a few
          singular products, made consistently, with secondary pastries curated from partners.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Ordering lane</th><th>Best use</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {lanes.map((l) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{l.lane}</strong></td><td>{l.use}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Order-ahead runs on a managed commerce platform (Square, Toast, or Lightspeed class)
          rather than custom software. Pricing is one list everywhere — walk-up, mobile, and
          drive-up — so guests never shop for a better online rate while standing in line. Every
          order, including online, is handed over at a dedicated staffed pickup counter, physically
          separate from the walk-up order queue: no lockers, no customer-facing kiosks, and a clear
          path so ahead-orders do not collide with people still deciding at the counter. The
          guest-facing incentive is that workflow — a kitchen pickup window, skip the order line,
          food ready when they arrive — and the internal payoff is clearer production planning.
          Preorder volume (target 15–25% of peak sales) lets the kitchen batch to known demand
          instead of guessing the line. Pickup windows belong to the kitchen, not to admission:
          they organize handoff timing without reserving a place on the grounds. Office and school
          bulk orders follow the Yates mechanic — minimum quantities, 48-hour lead time, volume
          pricing — making large orders a demand-smoothing channel instead of an afterthought.
        </p>

        <h3>Opening price architecture <small>(2030 dollars, to be validated in pilots)</small></h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Revenue stream</th><th class="num">Suggested pricing</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {pricing.map((p) => (
                <tr><td>{p.stream}</td><td class="num">{p.price}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Free admission is a deliberate contrast with Wiard’s $27.50 weekend gate and Blake’s
          $12–$14 festival pricing. The most common criticism of established venues — pay at the
          door, then pay again for everything inside — becomes Leaf & Lantern’s clearest
          differentiator. Ordering channels stay equally simple: one menu, one price list, three
          ways to buy — never a promotional rate that pulls walk-up guests onto their phones.
        </p>

        <h3>Three-year illustrative P&L <small>(thousands of dollars)</small></h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Year One</th><th class="num">Year Two</th><th class="num">Year Three</th></tr>
