              <tr><td>Depreciation</td><td class="num">(150)</td><td class="num">(150)</td><td class="num">(150)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Illustrative interest expense</td><td class="num">(130)</td><td class="num">(125)</td><td class="num">(120)</td></tr>
              <tr class="total"><td>Pre-tax profit / (loss)</td><td class="num">($262)</td><td class="num">($80)</td><td class="num">$103</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Two comparisons keep the Year-Three result honest. The $373K EBITDA headline is 16.2%
          of revenue, but after depreciation and illustrative interest the pre-tax profit is
          $103K — about 4.5% of revenue, close to the National Restaurant Association’s 4.0%
          median pre-tax margin for limited-service restaurants in 2024. The eventual net
          economics are not unusually rich; the aggressive assumptions are the transaction ramp
          and the flat payroll line, both of which are gated in the pilot program (see Unit
          Economics & Capacity). Before any financing, the model is stress-tested on four axes:
          food transactions at 40,000 / 55,000 / 70,000 / 85,000; average ticket at $15 / $17.50
          / $20; programs and education at 50% / 75% / 100% of plan; and payroll scaling with
          orders rather than remaining flat. The thesis must survive a weak-program case and a
          poor-autumn-weather case without adding weddings, a gate fee, or a larger attraction
          package.
        </p>

        <h3>Revenue build vs. cash break-even</h3>
