          recreates exactly the fixed-cost fragility this model is designed to avoid. No financing
          commitment is made until a contractor-led estimate and site-specific utility study
          replace these allowances.
        </p>

        <h3>Break-even definition and land</h3>
        <p>
          The stated cash break-even of <strong>≈70,000 annual food transactions (≈$2.0M
          revenue)</strong> is the level at which operating cash flow covers all operating
          expenses — including market-rate owner-manager compensation within payroll — plus
          interest and scheduled principal repayment. Illustratively, financing 65% of a $2.95M
          project at 7% over twenty years requires roughly $175K of annual debt service. Income
          taxes apply only above that level; depreciation is excluded as a non-cash item.
        </p>
        <p>
          The $2.95M startup allowance excludes land. A purchase scenario adds parcel acquisition
          to the capital requirement and its debt service to the break-even; a long-term
          ground-lease scenario adds an annual occupancy payment to the P&L instead. Both
          scenarios will be modeled with real numbers at site selection, and the financing
          structure will not be fixed before then.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Brand ============ -->
